ompanies  control much of our food brands so they  decide what kind of food we eat how much  we pay and how much damage is done but  there are solutions to this and and and  farmers organizations and presidents and  we as warren won't work with many of  them could be provo proposing these  solutions they say look we can cool this  planet and feed people we can call for a  global green new deal we can call for  food me made a fundamental right for  everyone but we can most importantly  tackle this food system by giving land  rights to those who actually produce the  food that feeds the world so it's not a  story just of doom and gloom but it is a  really important part of recognizing  that this climate crisis isn't simply  about fossil fuels it's also about the  future you're not shocked by it we'll  get on to more on to the solutions in a  moment but zapier i guess what this  shows is that climate change is now a  security risk just as war  absolutely um i think the critical point  i'd highlight to start off with is  really that this is a vicious cycle  climate change drives food insecurity  but our means of production the way that  we use our land and the way that we  produce  food is also driving the the climate  crisis so it is a vicious cycle just to  give you an example of that so for  example we know that between the year  2000 and 2018 and our agricultural  expansion actually drove 90 percent of  global deforestation which of course  drives climate change and you know  assad's already mentioned the the  industrialized agriculture model which  has been said to be responsible for  changing the world's surface more than  any other human activity so  that's on the one hand and then in terms  of how it's impacted and our food  production is impacted on by climate  change well unfortunately all we need to  do um is to look over at india where uh  the severe heat wave the hottest spring  on record has severely damaged the wheat  crops and that actually india was hoping  to to export some of which india was  hoping to export uh to help and mitigate  against the supply shock as a result of  the russia ukraine war so it really is  um a potentially vicious cycle and you  know the supply issue also obviously um  drives the high prices here we know that  you know fertilizers  need fossil fuels at the moment  predominantly  in order to be produced and that's  driving up the cost of fertilizers which  in turn  can cause another uh shock next year if  farmers can't afford to use  uh you know what what they've become  used to using because of this current  model of uh industrial agriculture  the fertilizers that they they they need  so it really is a potentially vicious  cycle here  is the future bleak i said do you feel  that the solutions are within reach  is there potential to change  so the solutions have always been within  reach and the solutions have been long  proposed and there are many  organizations that have been proposing  and we've worked for decades around this  issue of hunger and the broken food  system  and its impact on land on biodiversity  in people and so it's not a problem that  can't be fixed but like much of the  climate crisis what it relies on is  political will and political will to  take on some really powerful vested  interest  our food corporations are is about as  strong as the fossil fuel industry and  when we all know we need to end the  fossil fuels but yet we continue to  expand our fossil fuels and leading to  increasing temperature rise we have the  same situation with with food so how do  we change the dial well fundamentally  only we are going to be the people who  can move that make that happen if we  collectively start putting pressure  whether it's on our supermarkets  people power will be the key to this do  you agree briefly people power to change  the climate crisis  yeah i think it needs multiple solutions  really and i think the the reality is is  that we cannot simply try to adapt to  the in terms of food production to these  increasing temperatures we know uh that  uh crop yields will be significantly  impacted uh by rising temperatures so we  do need to really address  the the the carbon emissions and the  methane emissions that are driving a lot  of this crisis as well  okay so much more we could talk on that  subject um but we are out of time on  that question stay with me because i do  have  another topic we want to talk about  meditation now because it's  a mindfulness really it's something  completely different and it's a report  from the guardian newspaper that  officials in brussels are being trained  to meditate to help sort of tackle the  climate crisis it involves walking in  nature and developing mindfulness and  seppie i know you worked in whitehall  for  a while i believe um  do you think this is the solution  well i mean  one thing that used to strike me quite a  lot when i was i worked seven years in  whitehall and you know i always did  wonder you know when there was a flood  in yorkshire or uh you know somewhere  else around the country and then you had  officials sitting  in central london and making policies  for those places i remember thinking  well you know it's one thing to know  something  intellectually it's another thing to  actually experience it and if meditation  can actually help uh you know  officials try to you know go get out  outside of their own um environments  actually help them think more broadly  and it's been shown to increase empathy  then absolutely i think you know  go ahead anything that helps  how about you do you think this is the  key to tackling climate change or is it  all about action  well we need to do both right changing  the system isn't just about policies and  action as as if he said it's also about  empathy and solidarity and seeing  ourselves as part of the world so i  think to solve these problems of course  we need to speak to people's hearts as  well as their minds now look  you know one of the i i've worked at  both at the un at national level and one  of the biggest challenges we've got is  we've created an economic and political  system that promotes short-term economic  interests right it's all about profits  no matter what the cost we've created  ways of making decisions that put a  price on everything but often value  nothing so  we know that there is there is a  sickness in our in our general economy  right we consume more and more we're  greedy for more but we're still not  happy so how do we get across that well  i'm we're all in favor of all of these  bureaucrats walking through nature and  i'm glad they're doing that if they're  going to make better decisions but i'm  we all know that better decisions are  made by those who are mostly impacted  and i would love for them to actually  walk through nature in developing  countries where it's the corporations  and the policies of the eu that  responsible for that destruction or let  them go and actually live in the  communities that are most affected by  decisions and maybe we won't have such a  blase  approach to actually the seriousness of  tackling this climate crisis  assad and sepi thank you very much for  your insights into today's show  well on the show tomorrow we'll be  joined by founder of terror practices  kirsty gogan and co-founder of cop 26  action plan chidioti  but do stay with us because coming up  live from westminster at the take with  sophie ridge with interviews from all  the major parties ahead of tomorrow's  local elections thanks for watching  we'll see you next time bye  [Music]
 

